        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "Here\u2019s a **Mikuni-style Sea Steak** you can make at home. This is inspired by Mikuni\u2019s Japanese-fusion vibe showing off a sesame-crusted tuna steak with a light soy-ginger glaze. Clean, bold, restaurant-level flavor without overcomplicating it.\n\n\ue200image_group\ue202{\"query\":[\"sesame crusted tuna steak\",\"ahi tuna steak seared rare\",\"tuna steak soy ginger glaze\",\"sesame tuna plating\"]}\ue201\n\n## Mikuni-Inspired Sea Steak (Sesame-Crusted Ahi Tuna)\n\n### Ingredients (2 servings)\n\n**For the tuna**\n- 2 sushi-grade ahi tuna steaks (6\u20138 oz each, about 1.5 inches thick)\n- Salt and cracked black pepper\n- 2 tbsp neutral oil (avocado or grapeseed)\n\n**Sesame crust**\n- 2 tbsp white sesame seeds\n- 1 tbsp black sesame seeds\n\n**Soy-ginger glaze**\n- 3 tbsp low-sodium soy sauce or tamari\n- 1 tbsp mirin\n- 1 tsp rice vinegar\n- 1 tsp honey or sugar\n- 1 tsp fresh grated ginger\n- 1 small garlic clove, finely grated\n- Optional: a few drops of sesame oil\n\n**Garnish (optional but very Mikuni)**\n- Sliced scallions\n- Microgreens\n- Pickled ginger\n- Wasabi or yuzu kosho\n- Drizzle of spicy mayo or ponzu\n\n---\n\n### Instructions\n\n1. **Prep the tuna**  \n   Pat tuna completely dry. Season lightly with salt and pepper.\n\n2. **Crust it**  \n   Mix sesame seeds on a plate. Press tuna steaks into seeds on all sides so they adhere well.\n\n3. **Sear**  \n   Heat oil in a cast-iron or stainless pan over high heat until just smoking.  \n   Sear tuna 45\u201360 seconds per side for rare to medium-rare.  \n   You want a crust outside and cool center inside.\n\n4. **Make the glaze**  \n   While tuna rests, add all glaze ingredients to the same pan.  \n   Simmer 1\u20132 minutes until slightly syrupy. Do not overreduce.\n\n5. **Slice and plate**  \n   Slice tuna against the grain. Spoon glaze lightly over the top.  \n   Finish with scallions, microgreens, and any optional sauces.\n\n---\n\n### Pro Tips (very Mikuni energy)\n- Tuna should stay rare in the center. Overcooking kills the vibe.\n- Use sushi-grade fish only.\n- Keep plating minimal and elegant. White plate, negative space.\n- Add a citrus element like yuzu, lemon zest, or ponzu if you want brightness.\n\nIf you want, I can also:\n- Do a **salmon sea steak version**\n- Give you a **spicy Mikuni house sauce**\n- Pair it with **Asian-inspired sides** like garlic edamame or miso butter rice"
          ]
        },
